<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kartli</h1><p class="st-meta">1484 CE – 1762 CE · capital Tbilisi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The central Georgian kingdom around Tbilisi, a Persian vassal for much of its history, which produced Georgia's first printing press and a famous law code.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kartli</a>, the historic core of eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a>, emerged as a separate kingdom during the break-up of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a> in the late 15th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-ii-of-georgia">Constantine II</a>, the last king to claim all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, was left by about 1490 with Kartli alone, and his descendants ruled it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kartli</a> lay on the front line between the Ottoman and Safavid empires and was usually a Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>; several kings, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostom-of-kartli">Rostom</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to hold the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-i-of-kartli">Simon I</a> fought both empires and died an Ottoman captive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakhtang-vi-of-kartli">Vakhtang VI</a>, regent and king in the early 18th century, compiled a law code, edited the Georgian chronicles and set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 1709.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakhtang-vi-of-kartli">Vakhtang VI</a> sought Russian help and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1724, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kartli</a> until 1735. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teimuraz-ii-of-kakheti">Teimuraz II</a> regained the throne in 1744, and on his death in 1762 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> united Kartli with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti">Kakheti</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kartli.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kartli" width="320" height="211"><figcaption>Image: Goran tek-en · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgian_Kingdom_of_Kartli,_1490_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1484 CE – 1762 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tbilisi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Simon I, Rostom, Vakhtang VI</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kartli, Kartlian Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1623&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kartli" aria-label="Kingdom of Kartli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5771 691 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kartli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6091 947L6036 930M6091 947L6281 1235M6091 947L5709 970M6091 947L6298 1144M6091 947L6091 947M6091 947L6085 933M6091 947L6080 935"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6036 930h0M6281 1235h0M5709 970h0M6298 1144h0M6091 947h0M6085 933h0M6080 935h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tbilisi. <a href="/atlas?year=1623&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kartli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
